            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. PB2 Products and One Tested Alternative, Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three PB2 jars with live Amazon listings, plus two peanut butter flavored protein powders for comparison. Label figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor; prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 17 November 2026 and change often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-pb2" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which PB2 Products Are Actually Protein Powders?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only the PB2 Performance line is formulated as a protein powder; everything else PB2 sells is powdered peanut butter. The difference is not marketing nuance. Performance jars use a 45 g, two-scoop serving and add brown rice protein to the peanuts, while the powdered peanut butters use a 13 g, two-tablespoon serving and stop at 4 to 6 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            PB2 Foods started in 2007 with the original powdered peanut butter, which is roasted peanuts pressed to remove most of the oil, then ground and seasoned with a little sugar and salt. PB2's own product page says the powdered butters carry 4 to 6 g of protein per 13 g serving depending on the variety. That is a high protein share for a spread, about 46 percent of the weight, but nobody scoops 13 g of anything into a shaker bottle and calls it a protein shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The PB2 store currently lists the Performance range as Peanut Protein with Dutch Cocoa, Peanut Protein with Madagascar Vanilla and an Almond Protein with Vanilla. Each states 20 g of complete plant protein per serving, per PB2. Some grocery retailers, including Kroger and Vitacost, still list an unflavored PB2 Performance Pure Peanut Protein with 22 g of protein and 200 calories per 45 g, made from roasted peanuts alone. We could not find that version on PB2's own Performance page or on Amazon at the time of writing, so treat it as a retail-only item that may be on its way out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The powdered peanut butters are Original, Pure (no added sugar or salt), Cocoa, Crunchy, Organic, Organic Pure, Peanut Powder with Chocolate Chips, and a Powdered Almond Butter. They are useful products, and our <a href="/best-peanut-butter-protein-powder/">peanut butter protein powder guide</a> recommends them as a flavor booster. They just answer a different question than the one most people mean when they search for PB2 protein powder.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check the serving weight before the protein number.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 45 g serving with 20 g of protein is a protein powder. A 13 g serving with 6 g of protein is a pantry ingredient, however the jar is labeled on the front.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in PB2 Performance Peanut Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            PB2 Performance Peanut Protein with Dutch Cocoa contains six ingredients: roasted peanuts, brown rice protein, cocoa, inulin from Jerusalem artichoke, salt and monk fruit extract, per PB2's product page. The vanilla version swaps cocoa for natural flavors. There is no pea protein in either, which surprises some buyers who assume every plant powder is pea-based.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per the Vitacost label listing at the time of writing, a two-scoop (45 g) serving of the cocoa flavor gives 190 calories, 5 g of fat including 1.5 g saturated, 17 g of carbohydrate, 8 g of fiber, 4 g of total sugar with 0 g added, 20 g of protein and 210 mg of sodium. It also lists 2.6 mg of iron and 560 mg of potassium. PB2 states the vanilla flavor has the same 20 g of protein and 190 calories with 4.5 g of fat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pairing peanut with brown rice protein is a sensible choice. Peanut protein is relatively low in the amino acid methionine, and rice protein is relatively low in lysine, so a blend evens them out; PB2 markets the result as a complete protein. That is the same logic pea and rice blends use. What it does not do is make the powder lean: peanuts keep some fat even after pressing, which is why 20 g of protein costs 190 calories here, against roughly 100 to 130 calories in many pea isolates covered in our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein powder guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 8 g of fiber is the other number to notice. Most of it comes from inulin, a prebiotic fiber that PB2 adds on purpose. Inulin can help regularity, but it ferments in the gut and is a known trigger for gas and bloating in sensitive people, including many with IBS. If you have had trouble with chicory root fiber in protein bars, expect the same here and start with one scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweetness comes only from monk fruit extract, and there are no gums, oils or artificial flavors listed. Retail listings describe the cocoa jar as certified vegan, certified gluten-free, kosher and Non-GMO Project Verified. The allergen line reads simply: contains peanuts.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">PB2 Performance Cocoa, per 45 g serving</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein</p>
