        <div id="original-pure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Protein Is in PB2 Original and PB2 Pure?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Original PB2 has 6 g of protein per 2 tablespoon (13 g) serving, in 60 calories, per the label at the time of writing. PB2 Pure, the no-sugar, no-salt version, also lists 6 g of plant protein per serving. PB2 with Cocoa drops to 4 g per serving, because cocoa and sugar take up some of the weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the Original label is short. A 13 g serving has 1.5 g of fat, 5 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of fiber, 2 g of total sugar including 1 g of added sugar, and 90 mg of sodium, with about 70 servings in the 32 oz jar. Against regular peanut butter, PB2 says it has about 90 percent less fat and 70 percent fewer calories per serving, which is the whole point of the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sugar and salt look trivial per spoonful, and they are. The problem appears only if you try to use Original PB2 as your protein source. To reach 25 g of protein you need about 54 g of powder, a little over four servings. That dose brings roughly 250 calories, about 4 g of added sugar and about 375 mg of sodium. None of that is alarming, but it is more calories and more salt than a typical scoop of protein powder for the same protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            PB2 Pure fixes the sugar and salt by listing a single ingredient: roasted peanuts. For people who track added sugar, or who bake with PB2 and want to control sweetness themselves, Pure is the better jar. It tastes like plain roasted peanuts, so buyers who drink it straight in milk tend to add a sweetener or a banana.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful way to think about both jars is as a concentrated peanut ingredient with a helpful amount of protein. Two tablespoons added to a vanilla or chocolate shake makes it taste like peanut butter and adds 6 g of protein for 60 calories. That is a good trade, and it is how our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">baking protein guide</a> suggests using them in cookies, pancakes and protein balls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is PB2 Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            PB2 does not carry an independent third-party certification such as NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project on any product we checked. What PB2 does publish is a set of testing statements in its help center, and they are worth reading closely.
          </p>

          <p>
            On aflatoxin, the mold toxin that peanut buyers ask about most, PB2's help center says plainly: "Yes, we test for aflatoxin levels." On heavy metals, a separate PB2 article says the company "routinely tests its peanut flours and finished products against FDA and California Proposition 65 standards" for heavy metals and other contaminants, and that all ingredients and suppliers go through an approval and verification program. PB2 also says it operates under HACCP principles and the SQF food safety code.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those statements describe a food manufacturer's quality program, not a public third-party certificate. PB2 does not name the lab, say whether the testing is independent, or post batch certificates of analysis that we could find. That is common for food brands, and peanut processors are tightly regulated for aflatoxin in the US, but it is a lower bar than a supplement brand that publishes lot results or carries an NSF seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The certifications PB2 products do carry, per retail listings, are about diet rather than purity: certified vegan, certified gluten-free, kosher and Non-GMO Project Verified, plus USDA Organic on the two organic jars. None of these tests for banned substances or heavy metals.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you compete in a drug-tested sport, choose a powder with a banned-substance certification instead; the Vega card above is one peanut butter flavored option. For everyone else, PB2's aflatoxin and metals testing statements are reasonable reassurance for a food product. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">guide to protein powder safety</a> explains what each certification actually checks.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT PB2 SAYS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tested for aflatoxin and heavy metals, per PB2. Not third-party certified.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">PB2 states it tests for aflatoxin and checks metals against FDA and Prop 65 limits. It does not publish certificates of analysis or carry a sport certification.</p>
