        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly praise PB2 Performance for tasting like real peanut butter without a chemical aftertaste, and the most common complaints are grainy mixing and gas from the added fiber. We have not tasted it ourselves; the points below summarize reviews on PB2's own store and the retailer listings at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cocoa flavor carried 74 five-star ratings out of 85 on PB2's store when we checked. Reviewers describe it as not chalky, not too sweet and good in smoothies, overnight oats and frozen desserts made in a Ninja Creami. A few say it tastes more like peanut than chocolate; one buyer wrote that it tastes no different from regular PB2 and needed extra cacao to taste chocolatey. If you want a strong chocolate note, that is worth knowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing is the most consistent practical complaint. Because the base is ground peanut rather than a filtered isolate, it does not dissolve the way whey does. One reviewer said it stays clumpy or grainy however long they shake it; another recommends a milk frother or blender. Expect a thicker, slightly textured drink from a shaker bottle and a smoother one from a blender.
          </p>

          <p>
            Digestive reactions are the sharpest complaints. On the vanilla page, which had 32 five-star ratings out of 40 when we checked, two of the three one-star reviews described gas, bloating or loose stools, and one blamed the Jerusalem artichoke inulin directly. At the same time, one reviewer who is sensitive to pea protein said PB2 Performance was the first powder that did not upset her stomach. As for the best flavor, buyers lean toward cocoa for shakes and vanilla for mixing into yogurt, oats and baking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Original PB2 reviews focus on flavor and convenience rather than protein. Buyers like it in smoothies, oatmeal and as a drizzle mixed with water and maple syrup. One recurring critic argues it is really defatted peanut flour rather than peanut butter, which is a fair description of what is in the jar, and a few complaints concern shipping rather than the product.
          </p>
        </div>

