        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Original PB2 as a protein powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Original PB2 has 6 g of protein per 13 g serving, so a 25 g dose takes about 54 g of powder and 250 calories. It is excellent for flavor. If the goal is a protein shake, buy PB2 Performance, which gives 20 g in one 45 g serving, or use Original alongside another powder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming PB2 Performance is pea protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                PB2 Performance is roasted peanuts plus brown rice protein, with no pea protein on the label. That is good news for people who react to pea, but it also means more fat and calories than pea isolates. Read the ingredient list rather than assuming every vegan powder shares the same base.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking a full serving of inulin on day one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each Performance serving contains about 8 g of fiber, much of it inulin from Jerusalem artichoke. Some buyers report gas and bloating, and blame the fiber. Start with one scoop for a week, drink it with food, and build up once you know how your gut handles it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating PB2's testing statements as a sport certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                PB2 says it tests for aflatoxin and checks heavy metals against FDA and Prop 65 limits. That is a food-safety program, not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Drug-tested athletes need a powder whose banned-substance certification can be looked up in the certifier's database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking it in a bottle and expecting a smooth drink</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The base is ground peanut, so it never fully dissolves like whey. Buyers report a grainy or clumpy texture from shaker bottles. A blender, a milk frother, or mixing it into oats, yogurt or batter gives a much better result and avoids wasting a jar over texture.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

